  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 120 PM CDT SAT OCT 31 2026\n\nThis weekend: With further vertical moistening and the presence\nof a few shortwave trough axes, precipitation chances are favored\nto gradually increase. Spotty activity is forecast during trick-\nor-treat, leading up to a wetter overnight into Sunday. A\nmajority of this activity will be in the form of showers due to\nlow-level stability; however, a couple rumbles of thunder may\noccur given instances of weak MU-CAPE. Recent runs of various\nmodels and the 12z ensemble show the potential for pockets of\nmoderate to heavy rain northwest of the 59/20 Sunday morning, \nassociated with a mid-level lobe of energy.\n\nNext week: Much of the upcoming workweek will feature increased\ncloudiness as additional plumes of moisture aloft come our way\nfrom the west. Monday and Tuesday look to be free from rain as a\nshortwave ridge moves across the Gulf region. Our next system\ncurrently exists as a pair of troughs and jet streaks just off\nthe west coast of the U.S. and Mexico. Models have been\nconsistent in showing these troughs interacting/phasing as they\ntrack from the Desert Southwest to the Plains and arrive to our\narea on Wednesday, though there's variability in the\nconfiguration and intensity of the system by that point. At this\ntime, the various model solutions, along with ML/AI guidance,\ndon't seem to point toward a robust severe thunderstorm threat\ngiven a few sizable limiting factors; however, given background\nshear and some instability, a low severe thunderstorm risk may\nmaterialize. Heavy rain may also occur with this system for parts\nof the region. We'll narrow down each as we get closer.\n\n89^GSatterwhite",
